  "summary": "Ottoman copper-alloy vessels gilded warm as gold, from palace ewers to dervish bowls.",
  "description": "Tombak — the Ottoman copper-zinc alloy gilded to a soft gold glow — served wherever silver was barred or impractical: palace coffee sets, sherbet jugs, hand-washing ewers and basins, dervish kashkul bowls, mirror frames and mosque fittings. Topkapı workshops and provincial coppersmiths hammered, chased and inlaid the metal with elegant tulip and carnation handles, faceted bodies and calligraphic bands. The warm gilt sheen defined Ottoman table ceremony, and surviving pieces still anchor Turkish and European collections.",
